<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Luisa, Duchess of Lucca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1782–1824</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Maria Luisa of Spain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Luisa was a daughter of Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-etruria">Louis of Parma</a>, made king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1801. After his death in 1803 she ruled Etruria as regent for their son Charles Louis until Napoleon ended the kingdom in 1807. As compensation for Parma, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave her the Duchy of Lucca, which she ruled from 1817 until her death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-luisa-duchess-of-lucca.jpg" alt="Maria Luisa, Duchess of Lucca" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François-Xavier Fabre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maria_Luisa_of_Spain,_queen_of_Etruria_and_duchess_of_Lucca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
